<article>
    <h1>{$selectedSection}</h1>
    <section>
        <p>
            Projectile motion is a motion in plane with constant acceleration due to gravity. An object is said to be <em>projected</em> in this case. All we need to do is to substitute {`\\(a_x = 0, a_y = -g\\)`} in the equations of motion obtained in the previous section. The equations of motion of a projectile consist of two sets of equations of motion in straight line, one along {`\\(x\\)`}-axis and another along {`\\(y\\)`}-axis. We can write them as follows:
        </p>
        <table style="margin:auto;">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Horizontal motion</th>
                    <th>Vertical motion</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tr>
                <td style="border-right:1px solid;">
                    {`\\[
                        \\begin{eqnarray}    
                            v_x &=& u_x\\\\
                            x &=& u_xt\\\\
                            v_x^2 &=& u_x^2
                        \\end{eqnarray}
                        \\]
                        `}
                </td>
                <td>
                    {`\\[
                        \\begin{eqnarray}    
                            v_y &=& u_y -gt\\\\
                            y &=& u_yt - \\frac{1}{2}gt^2\\\\
                            v_y^2 &=& u_y^2 - 2gy
                        \\end{eqnarray}
                        \\]
                        `}
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <p>where {`\\(\\vec{r},\\vec{u}, \\vec{v}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} have been <em>resolved</em> into their {`\\(x\\)`} and {`\\(y\\)`} components. </p>
        {`\\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}    
                \\vec{r} &=& x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j}\\\\
                \\vec{u} &=& u_x\\hat{i} + u_y\\hat{j}\\\\
                \\vec{v} &=& v_x\\hat{i} + v_y\\hat{j}\\\\
                \\vec{a} &=& - g\\hat{j}
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]
            `}
