    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Time of Flight</h2>
        <p>How does the object remain above the ground? This time is called the time of flight. At the start of the flight, the initial velocity along the {`\\(y\\)`}-axis is {`\\(u_y = u\\sin\\theta\\)`} and the final velocity when it hits the ground is {`\\(v_y = -u\\sin\\theta\\)`}. Let {`\\(T\\)`} be the time taken. Then {`\\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}
                v_y &=& u_y -gT\\\\\
                -u\\sin\\theta &=& u\\sin\\theta - gT\\\\
                T &=& \\frac{2u\\sin\\theta}{g}
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]`} </p>
        <p>Therefore, the object remains in the air for time {`\\(T= \\dfrac{2u\\sin\\theta}{g}\\)`}</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Range</h2>
        <p>How far does the object land in the horizontal direction? This one is easy given the time of flight {`\\(T\\)`}. {`\\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}
                R &=& u_xT\\\\\
                  &=& u\\cos\\theta\\frac{2u\\sin\\theta}{g}\\\\
                  &=& \\frac{u^2\\sin2\\theta}{g}
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]`}
        </p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Maximum height</h2>
        <p>The object hits the maximum height after {`\\(\\dfrac{T}{2}\\)`}. {`\\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}
                H &=& u_y\\frac{T}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}g\\Big[\\frac{T}{2}\\Big]^2\\\\\
                  &=& u\\sin\\theta\\frac{2u\\sin\\theta}{2g}-\\frac{1}{2}g\\Big[\\frac{2u\\sin\\theta}{2g}\\Big]^2\\\\
                  &=& \\frac{u^2\\sin^2\\theta}{2g}
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]`}</p>
    </section>
